WEBVTT

NOTE CHAPTER 01 · The brief is the first part
1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.250
Every car begins with a job. Define what it must do before drawing hardware.

2
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:32.500
Fast, cheap, and safe are wishes until each becomes a measurable question.

3
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:48.750
A useful target states a quantity, unit, condition, and verification method. The 1,200 kg target is illustrative.

4
00:00:48.750 --> 00:01:05.000
Make each requirement testable. NASA’s systems engineering handbook informs the method, not vehicle law.

NOTE CHAPTER 02 · Draw the system boundary
1
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:23.750
Set the boundary around the complete vehicle system.

2
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:42.500
Separate the layers to expose the interfaces between subsystems.

3
00:01:42.500 --> 00:02:01.250
Trace energy, force, information, and heat across those interfaces.

4
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:20.000
Reconnect the system map and inspect the unresolved links.

NOTE CHAPTER 03 · Make a mass budget
1
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Component mass figures are estimates; keep their sources and uncertainty.

2
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:00.000
These illustrative subsystem estimates sum to 1,200 kilograms.

3
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:20.000
At a stated 2 metres per second squared, F equals m a: 2,400 newtons.

4
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:40.000
This first-order net-force check omits road loads and tire limits.

NOTE CHAPTER 04 · Trace energy before choosing power
1
00:03:40.000 --> 00:04:00.000
A force acting along a distance transfers work in this simple model.

2
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:20.000
100 newtons through 20 metres is 2,000 joules of work.

3
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Deliver 2,000 joules in 10 seconds: average power is 200 watts.

4
00:04:40.000 --> 00:05:00.000
This idealized example does not size a motor or powertrain.

NOTE CHAPTER 05 · Give the shape an air-resistance check
1
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Drag depends on air density, speed, reference area, and shape.

2
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Read the variable meanings and units before using the drag equation.

3
00:05:40.000 --> 00:06:00.000
With other values fixed, doubling 20 to 40 metres per second makes drag four times larger.

4
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:20.000
Illustrative inputs are not vehicle data; real airflow needs validated testing.

NOTE CHAPTER 06 · Resolve the design conflicts
1
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:38.750
Vehicle objectives interact; a local improvement can create a new penalty.

2
00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:57.500
This schematic trade space shows alternatives, not measured vehicle scores.

3
00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:16.250
Safety and applicable legal requirements are constraints to verify.

4
00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:35.000
Select against the brief and preserve evidence, assumptions, and uncertainty.

NOTE CHAPTER 07 · Build a verification and law checklist
1
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:53.750
A plausible calculation does not certify a vehicle for road use.

2
00:07:53.750 --> 00:08:12.500
Requirements depend on jurisdiction, vehicle type, modification, and use.

3
00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:31.250
NHTSA and UNECE are examples of official frameworks, not universal rules.

4
00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:50.000
Check current local authority requirements; this lesson makes no legal determination.

NOTE CHAPTER 08 · Review the brief before the first build
1
00:08:50.000 --> 00:09:07.500
Trace each design target to a source, calculation, or planned test.

2
00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:25.000
Keep assumptions and uncertainty connected to every estimate.

3
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:42.500
Assign an explicit next verification step to every important unknown.

4
00:09:42.500 --> 00:10:00.000
The brief is ready for the next study: vehicle layout and packaging.
